import std::list;

// The three structures the musical domains share, named once.
//
// Every one of them was already in the library, written by hand under a
// different name: `interval_add` and `interval_inverse` are a group, and
// `pitch_transposed` beside `pitchclass_transposed` is one action spelled
// twice. Naming them is not abstraction for its own sake — it is what lets
// a reader recognize in one module the structure they already read in
// another, and what stops the same operation acquiring a third spelling in
// a fourth place.
//
// The laws each one is expected to satisfy are prose here and law suites in
// `docs/rules/language/05-verification.md` §4. A record in this language
// carries fields and never obligations: an obligation needs evidence, and
// evidence needs a term witnessing an equality.
//
// # Why no field below holds a law
//
// The sentence above was written when there was no evidence to attach.
// There is now — prompt 156 made `Equal` declarable and prompt 157 made a
// structure an ordinary record, so a field *may* be an equation and a
// record literal that does not satisfy it is refused at the literal. Prompt
// 163 went looking for one to add and found none. The reason is worth
// stating here rather than in a note, because it is a fact about this
// module's carriers and someone will otherwise try again:
//
// **A δ-rule computes on canonical data, so it does not step under a
// constructor.** `nat_add` is a compiler builtin — note 61 records why it
// stays one — and `x + 0` is stuck for a `x` the checker only knows to be a
// `Nat`. Splitting it does not help: the `Succ` arm is stuck too, on
// `nat_add (Succ i) 0`. Every operation on every carrier here reaches that
// arithmetic before it reaches a normal form — `Cyclic(n)` through
// `reduced_in`, `Ti` through `plus_in` and `minus_in`, `Pc(n)` through both
// — so a quantified law over any of them has no inhabitant an author can
// write, and a field typed by one could never be filled.
//
// Three things this is *not*, each measured rather than assumed:
//
// - **Not the record mechanism.** A field whose type mentions an earlier
//   field is checked against the field the literal supplied, and a false
//   equation is refused there.
// - **Not `Equal`, and not the absence of tactics.** Induction goes through
//   whenever the operation is written by matching. Writing
//   `fn cong({A: Type}, {B: Type}, f: A -> B, x: A, y: A, same: Equal(A, x, y)) -> Equal(B, f(x), f(y))`
//   as one `match` on `Refl` type-checks, and with it a proof of
//   `plus(x, Zero) = x` by induction on `x` type-checks, for a `plus`
//   written the way `number_of` below is.
// - **Not the surface's missing named function type.** `(x: G) -> …` does
//   not parse, which is a real gap and `std::indexed`'s `row_top` is the
//   other evidence for it. It is not this one: a spelling for a field that
//   has no inhabitant would have nothing to hold.
//
// And one thing it is, for `Ti` in particular: the T/I group's laws are
// **false** up to `Equal` and true after applying the labels to a chosen
// cycle. `Ti` is deliberately not indexed by the cycle, so `Transpose(15)`
// and `Transpose(3)` are one operation at twelve and two terms anywhere. A
// proposition about labels is the wrong proposition; comparing their action
// on the carrier is the right one.
//
// What *does* compute is every closed instance. `ti_compose(12, chromatic,
// Transpose(0), Transpose(3))` is `Transpose(3)` by evaluation, and the
// `Equal` saying so is inhabited by `Refl` today — which is what the
// fixtures in `examples/` are made of. A law is the quantified form of
// exactly those, and quantifying is the step that does not survive.
//
// # Records, and what the operations are
//
// Each structure is a record, so it is a *value*: a function may take two
// groups, return one, or hold one in a list, none of which the interface
// these replaced could do. What a record cannot do is decide which
// operation a call means — and nothing has to, because the operations live
// in each carrier's own namespace. `P5.unit()` is `Interval.unit(P5)` and
// `c4.act(M3)` is `Pitch.act(c4, M3)`, each an ordinary definition found by
// the head of the receiver's type.

// A set with an associative composition, a unit, and inverses.
//
// Laws: `compose(unit(g), g)` and `compose(g, unit(g))` are `g`; composition
// is associative; `compose(g, inverse(g))` is `unit(g)`.
//
// Not every set of movers is one. A duration composes and has a zero and
// has no inverse, because a duration is a length and not a displacement,
// and `Action` below is stated so that such a mover is still admitted.
//
// `unit` takes an element, which looks redundant and is not: the element is
// read for its *type* and never for its value, so `P5.unit()` is `P1` and so
// is `m2.unit()`. Written as a field it is what a caller of the record
// supplies; written as `Interval.unit` it is what `P5.unit()` finds.
record Group(G: Type) {
    // The element that changes nothing.
    unit: G -> G;

    // Apply the second, then the first. The order is the one `std::core`'s
    // `compose` already uses, so a reader who knows that knows this.
    compose: G -> G -> G;

    // The element that undoes this one.
    inverse: G -> G;
}

// A carrier moved by a set of movers.
//
// Laws: `act(x, unit(g))` is `x`, and `act(x, compose(g, h))` is `act(act(x,
// h), g)`. Both are stated over whatever unit and composition the mover
// has, so a monoid acting is an action here and not an approximation of
// one; where the mover is also a `Group`, the action is invertible and the
// second law reads backwards as well as forwards.
//
// The second law is what makes a transformation an object rather than a
// call: if composing two movers and acting once agrees with acting twice,
// a chain can be built before anything is applied.
//
// **The carrier comes first** (`docs/rules/style-guide.md` §6). One mover
// moves several carriers — an interval moves both a pitch and a spelled
// pitch class — and the carrier is what a reader is asking about, so it is
// what the parameter list says first. It is also where `x.act(g)` looks:
// the receiver decides which `act` runs.
record Action(X: Type, G: Type) {
    // Move one member of the carrier by one mover.
    act: X -> G -> X;
}

// A carrier on which the movers act *simply transitively*: every ordered
// pair of points is joined by exactly one mover.
//
// That uniqueness is the whole content, so `difference` is the whole record.
// The movement itself is an `Action`'s `act` and is not declared twice; the
// law joining them is `act(a, difference(a, b))` is `b`, for every `a` and
// `b`.
//
// **Two points never add.** `a + b` for two pitches or two positions is the
// single arithmetic error the point/mover split exists to catch, so there
// is no field here that could spell it.
record Torsor(P: Type, V: Type) {
    // The mover carrying `start` to `finish`. Unique — that is the claim.
    difference: P -> P -> V;
}

// Written musical time, moved by a written length.
//
// An action and deliberately **not** a torsor: `position_between` refuses a
// second position that stands before the first, because a duration is a
// length rather than a displacement, so the movers have no inverses and two
// positions in the wrong order are joined by nothing. The difference
// between a position and a later one is asked for by name —
// `position_between` — where the caller can see the order it must supply.
//
// At written time, and not at clock time. `Position(PhysicalTime)` is a
// performance coordinate and the two do not unify, which is roadmap §2's
// layer table; a second `act` at that coordinate would be one word claiming
// the pair is one structure. The namespace is keyed on `Position` alone, so
// the coordinate is written where it belongs — in the signature.
impl Position {
    fn act(subject: Position(WrittenTime), operation: Duration(WrittenTime)) -> Position(
        WrittenTime,
    ) { position_shift(subject, operation) }
}

// # Orbit and stabilizer, once for every finite action
//
// Two questions a finite group action answers, and the two that
// `52-the-musical-algebra.md` §2.3 found three compiler builtins asking one
// modulus at a time. A set class is an orbit of pitch-class sets under the
// T/I group; Messiaen's modes of limited transposition are exactly the
// collections whose stabilizer under transposition is nontrivial; a row's
// symmetries are its stabilizer. Written here, once, they are the same two
// functions for a bell pattern in a cycle of twelve pulses and for a
// twenty-four-tone row.
//
// Both take the group as a list, because a *finite* action is what makes
// them computable at all: `03-musical-domains.md` §5's "what the index buys
// is counted, not claimed" is exactly this — once the carrier's size is
// known the group can be enumerated, and enumeration is an ordinary fold.
//
// Both take equality on the carrier as an argument rather than finding it.
// A record carries fields and never obligations, so a `Group` that also
// promised decidable equality on its carrier would be promising something
// no field of it holds; and the caller always has the equality, because the
// caller is what knew the carrier was finite.

// Whether `built` already holds a member equal to `value`.
private fn already({X: Type}, same: X -> X -> Bool, built: List(X), value: X) -> Bool {
    any(fn (seen: X) -> Bool { same(seen, value) }, built)
}

// `built`, with `value` at its front where it is not already there.
//
// Prepended, and the fold's answer reversed once at the end. Appending
// instead would rebuild the whole list at every new member, which is a
// second quadratic on top of the comparison's — and at a division of twelve
// the row group has forty-eight members, where a spare quadratic is the step
// budget. The order a caller sees is unchanged: the group's own, first
// occurrence winning, which is what makes a canonical representative a
// question about the answer rather than about this function.
private fn adding({X: Type}, same: X -> X -> Bool, built: List(X), value: X) -> List(X) {
    if already(same, built, value) {
        built
    } else { Cons(value, built) }
}

// The distinct images of `point` under every operation in `group`.
//
// Order is the group's own, first occurrence winning, and duplicates are
// dropped — which is what makes the *length* of this the count that
// `row12_forms` was: how many distinct rows the labelled forms produce.
fn orbit(
    {X: Type},
    {G: Type},
    action: Action(X, G),
    same: X -> X -> Bool,
    group: List(G),
    point: X,
) -> List(X) {
    reverse(
        group.fold_from_start(
            [],
            fn (built: List(X), by: G) -> List(X) { adding(same, built, action.act(point, by)) },
        ),
    )
}

// The operations in `group` that leave `point` where it is.
//
// Its length times the orbit's is the size of the group, which is the
// orbit-stabilizer accounting `110-row-properties.md` asks the four form
// labels to do on their own.
fn stabilizer(
    {X: Type},
    {G: Type},
    action: Action(X, G),
    same: X -> X -> Bool,
    group: List(G),
    point: X,
) -> List(G) { filter(fn (by: G) -> Bool { same(action.act(point, by), point) }, group) }
